\strong{A refusal never becomes an empty layer.} A throttled instance answers with
well-formed XML of a few hundred bytes, no HTTP error code, and a \verb{<remark>}
element. Read naively, that says \emph{"nothing here"} -- the mistake that hid the
absence of DFCI data for a whole day.

\strong{Why \code{curl} and not \code{osmdata}.} A saturated instance does not error, it
\emph{stalls}: \code{osmdata} then loops on a 60 s backoff with no ceiling (16
consecutive retries measured, 16 minutes of pure waiting). \code{setTimeLimit()}
cannot help -- it only fires at R interpreter checkpoints, never on a socket
blocked inside C. The bound must live in the transport, and libcurl's
\code{timeout} provides exactly that.

\strong{Why instance rotation used to fail.} \code{osmdata::set_overpass_url()} calls
\code{overpass_status()}, so \emph{switching instances is itself a network call}: when
the instance is saturated it is the switch that fails, and rotation dies
before trying a single mirror. Passing the URL as a loop argument removes the
problem by construction.
}
\section{Bounded duration}{

No call can exceed \code{timeout * length(serveurs) * (1 + max_reprises)} seconds
-- 90 x 4 x 3 = \strong{18 minutes} worst case with the defaults, and a few seconds
nominally. Lower \code{timeout} or shorten \code{serveurs} to tighten it. On HTTP 429 a
\code{Retry-After} header is honoured \strong{only if under 10 s}; beyond that, moving to
the next instance beats waiting.
}

